          "type": "data",
          "data": {
            "text/markdown": "<span class=\"markdown prose dark:prose-invert contents\"><span class=\"paragraph\"><img style=\"float: right; margin: 10px\" width=\"300\" src=\"https://static0.srcdn.com/wordpress/wp-content/uploads/2025/02/christopher-lloyd-s-doc-brown-stares-at-the-camera-in-back-to-the-future.jpg?q=70&fit=crop&w=1568&h=1078&dpr=1\" title=\"Universal Pictures\"></span>\n<span class=\"paragraph\">We've been approached by a client who needs us to help him <strong>design a fusion reactor to power his time machine</strong>. He seems a little chaotic but his enthusiasm is infectious and we agree to help him in a quest of scientific and engineering exploration.</span>\n<span class=\"paragraph\">He has previously used nuclear fission, so we know his time travelling device works. However, he can't get his hands on any more Plutonium and would prefer a more powerful and safer device.</span>\n<span class=\"paragraph\">He has a couple of requirements,</span>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>1.8 GW</strong> of fusion power</li>\n<li>As small a device as we can manage</li>\n</ol>\n<span class=\"paragraph\">From our new fusion expertise, we know that a Spherical Tokamak will be our best bet at achieving a small machine.</span></span>"
          }
